ment when needed and gradual mobilisation. 3. CONGENITAL HEART DISEASE SURGERIES Definition Congenital heart disease (CHD) means a structural abnormality of the heart present from birth. The source states that abnormalities usually arise during the third to eighth week of gestation. filecite turn12file0 L25-L32 Types • Acyanotic lesions – e.g. PDA, ASD and VSD. • Cyanotic lesions – e.g. Tetralogy of Fallot and transposition of the great arteries. Common operations • PDA: closure by interventional device or surgical ligation/division when appropriate. • VSD: surgical closure, often with a patch, when significant. • TOF: repair closes the VSD and relieves right-ventricular outflow obstruction; palliation may precede definitive repair. • Transposition: temporary mixing may be supported; definitive repair is commonly an arterial switch operation. The source specifically describes PDA closure and the risks of untreated significant PDA. filecite turn12file2 L127-L158 Indications • Significant shunt causing symptoms or cardiac overload. • Heart failure or significant pulmonary over-circulation. • Cyanosis or important obstruction. • Risk of pulmonary vascular disease or other major complications. Contraindications / limitations The supplied pages do not provide a universal contraindication list. Advanced irreversible pulmonary vascular disease can make closure of a long-standing shunt inappropriate; individual anatomy and operative risk determine timing. Complications • Bleeding and infection. • Arrhythmias. • Residual shunt or obstruction. • Pulmonary hypertension. • Low cardiac output and organ complications. Management Pre-op: echocardiography and other indicated investigations, assess oxygenation, pulmonary pressure, nutrition and heart failure. Medical: manage heart failure/hypoxaemia; selected duct-dependent lesions may require prostaglandin support. Post-op: monitor SpO2, rhythm, perfusion, drains, pain, respiration and fluid status. PT: positioning, gentle respiratory care, secretion clearance when indicated and gradual age-appropriate mobility. 4. CORONARY ARTERY BYPASS GRAFTING (CABG) Definition CABG is a surgical method of revascularisation in which a graft is connected beyond a narrowed coronary artery to create a new route for blood flow. Indications • Persistent limiting angina/angina-equivalent symptoms despite optimal medical therapy. • Certain high-risk coronary anatomical patterns where CABG provides prognostic benefit. • Important left-main or multivessel disease in appropriate patients. • Selected patients with impaired ventricular function and significant coronary disease. Page 3 Cardiac Surgeries | Easy-language notesThe source states that CABG is justified for persistent limiting symptoms despite optimal medical therapy and/or certain anatomical patterns. filecite turn11file9 L587-L602 Contraindications / limitations The source frames CABG selection as a balance of expected benefit versus risk and CABG versus PCI; it does not give an absolute contraindication list. Unsuitable coronary anatomy or prohibitive operative risk may make CABG inappropriate. Types • On-pump CABG – uses CPB. • Off-pump CABG – beating-heart surgery with stabilisation. • MIDCAB – minimally invasive direct CABG in selected patients. The source describes off-pump and MIDCAB approaches. filecite turn12file5 L324-L344 Grafts / conduits • LIMA/internal thoracic artery: conduit of choice for LAD in the source. • Long saphenous vein: common venous conduit. • Radial artery: may be used; Allen's test checks collateral hand circulation before harvest. Source: filecite turn12file8 L546-L560 Procedure • Preoperative assessment and monitoring. • Harvest saphenous vein/radial artery and dissect LIMA when selected. • Median sternotomy for conventional CABG. • Heparinise and establish CPB for on-pump surgery. • Cross-clamp aorta and arrest/protect the heart with cardioplegia. • Attach grafts distal to the coronary stenoses. • Reperfuse, complete proximal anastomoses when required, warm and wean from CPB. • Reverse heparin and transfer to ICU. Source operative sequence: filecite turn12file3 L194-L215 Complications • Bleeding; rarely early tamponade requiring emergency resternotomy. • Atrial fibrillation and other arrhythmias. • Low cardiac output. • Respiratory, neurological, renal and wound complications. The source reports significant bleeding in about 2–3% and atrial fibrillation around 30% after CABG. filecite turn12file4 L276-L300 Management Pre-op: assess IHD severity/stability, valves, LV function, carotid/peripheral disease, respiratory status, diabetes, renal function, coagulation and medications. filecite turn12file8 L511-L525 Medical: antiplatelet/statin/anti-anginal therapy and risk-factor control as prescribed; perioperative medication changes are surgeon-directed. Post-op: ICU monitoring, pain control, wound/drain care, rhythm and cardiac-output management. PT: breathing exercises, thoracic expansion, supported cough, secretion clearance when indicated, bed mobility → sitting → standing → walking, followed by cardiac rehabilitation. 5. ANEURYSM / AORTIC SURGERY Definition An aneurysm is an abnormal dilatation of a blood vessel. In the supplied chapter, thoracic aortic aneurysm/dissection is the relevant cardiac-surgery topic. Types Page 4 Cardiac Surgeries | Easy-language notes• Ascending aortic aneurysm. • Aortic arch aneurysm. • Descending thoracic aneurysm. • Aortic dissection – Stanford type A involves the ascending aorta; type B does not. Indications • Symptoms or progressive enlargement. • Ascending aneurysm: progressive aortic valve insufficiency, symptoms, and in selected Marfan-related cases a diameter around 5–6 cm. • Descending aneurysm: symptoms, acute enlargement or diameter around 6 cm. Source indications: filecite turn11file6 L399-L412 Contraindications / limitations The supplied source does not give a general contraindication list. Treatment depends on location, size, symptoms, growth, comorbidities and operative risk. Procedure • Approach depends on location; typically median sternotomy and CPB for relevant ascending/aortic arch operations. • Cooling may be used before cross-clamping the aorta. • Diseased segment may be replaced with a synthetic/composite graft. • Selected descending dissections may be managed medically or with endovascular/surgical approaches depending on complications. Source: filecite turn11file6 L406-L412 ; type-B dissection management: filecite turn11file3 L185-L200 Complications • Rupture and major haemorrhage. • Cardiac tamponade. • Stroke/neurological injury. • Spinal cord ischaemia/paraplegia. • Renal, intestinal or limb ischaemia. Management Pre-op: CT/MRI, cardiac assessment and evaluation of comorbidities/coronary disease. Medical: blood-pressure control is central, especially in type-B dissection when initially managed medically. filecite turn11file3 L189-L200 Post-op: ICU haemodynamic, neurological, renal and respiratory monitoring. PT: respiratory care and gradual mobilisation after medical/surgical stability; avoid excessive strain and follow surgical precautions. 6. CARDIAC TAMPONADE MANAGEMENT Definition Cardiac tamponade occurs when pericardial fluid or blood raises pressure around the heart, compressing it, reducing venous return and compromising circulation. The source describes low BP, raised JVP and paradoxical pulse as important clinical features. filecite turn11file2 L140-L158 Types / causes • Acute tamponade – may occur rapidly after trauma, coronary angiography or surgery. • Chronic tamponade – may result from malignant pericardial infiltration, uraemia or connective-tissue disease. Indication for urgent treatment • Haemodynamic compromise/shock due to tamponade. • Rapid accumulation of pericardial blood/fluid. Page 5 Cardiac Surgeries | Easy-language notesContraindications / limitations The source does not give formal contraindications. In life-threatening tamponade, drainage is urgent; the method depends on cause and clinical setting. Procedure / management • Emergency: aspiration/drainage of the pericardial space (pericardiocentesis). • Penetrating cardiac wounds usually require surgical exploration through median sternotomy. • Chronic tamponade may require a pericardial window. Source: filecite turn11file0 L11-L33 Complications • Severe hypotension and shock. • Cardiac arrest. • Organ hypoperfusion and death if untreated. PT PT is not the primary treatment. After definitive treatment and haemodynamic stabilisation: breathing exercises, positioning and gradual mobilisation with close monitoring. 7. HEART TRANSPLANT Definition Heart transplantation is replacement of a severely diseased heart with a donor heart. Important source limitation: the supplied pages 823–849 do not provide a detailed heart-transplant section, so the following is general study information rather than a direct extraction. Indications • Selected end-stage heart failure despite optimal treatment. • Severe cardiac disease with poor expected survival/quality of life. Contraindications / limitations • Uncontrolled active infection. • Certain active malignancies. • Severe irreversible dysfunction of other major organs. • Conditions preventing safe lifelong immunosuppression/follow-up. Procedure • Donor and recipient assessment. • Recipient supported with CPB. • Diseased heart removed and donor heart implanted. • Major vascular/atrial connections are completed. • Reperfusion and weaning from CPB. Complications • Primary graft dysfunction. • Acute/chronic rejection. • Infection due to immunosuppression. • Renal and drug-related complications. • Coronary allograft vasculopathy. Management Pre-op: transplant assessment and optimisation. Medical: lifelong immunosuppression and infection/rejection surveillance. Post-op: ICU monitoring and rehabilitation. PT: respiratory care, early mobilisation and progressive aerobic conditioning according to transplant-team precautions. Page 6 Cardiac Surgeries | Easy-language notes8. CARDIAC SHUNTS Definition A cardiac shunt is an abnormal communication that allows blood to pass between cardiac chambers or vessels. Types • Left-to-right: oxygenated blood returns to pulmonary circulation; examples include ASD, VSD and PDA. • Right-to-left: deoxygenated blood enters systemic circulation and may cause cyanosis. • Fetal shunts: ductus venosus, foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus are normal fetal pathways. The supplied source describes these three fetal shunts and their closure after birth. filecite turn12file0 L33-L57 Common surgical/interventional treatment • PDA – device closure or surgical ligation/division. • VSD – patch closure when indicated. • Selected cyanotic CHD – palliative systemic-to-pulmonary shunt may be used. • Transposition – atrial septostomy may temporarily improve mixing; definitive arterial switch is used when appropriate. Indications • Significant shunt causing heart failure or pulmonary over-circulation. • Cyanosis/right-to-left shunting. • Large defects with important haemodynamic effects. Contraindications / limitations Long-standing shunt with irreversible pulmonary vascular disease may no longer be suitable for closure. Complications • Heart failure. • Pulmonary hypertension. • Arrhythmias. • Residual shunt. • Cyanosis/Eisenmenger physiology in advanced untreated disease. Management Pre-op: echo and haemodynamic assessment as indicated. Medical: manage heart failure/hypoxaemia and selected duct-dependent circulation. Post-op: monitor oxygenation, rhythm, perfusion and fluid balance. PT: respiratory support and graded activity after stabilisation. 9. GENERAL PRE-OP + POST-OP + MEDICAL + PT MANAGEMENT Pre-operative management • Complete history and physical assessment. • ECG, echocardiography, coronary angiography/CT/MRI or other tests according to operation. • Assess LV function, respiratory status, renal function, diabetes and coagulation. • Review all medications; perioperative stopping/continuation is surgeon-directed. • Patient education: breathing exercises, supported cough, early mobility and precautions. Post-operative management • ICU monitoring: HR, BP, SpO2, rhythm, urine output and drains. • Pain control and wound/sternal care. • Respiratory support and prevention of atelectasis/secretion retention. • Monitor for bleeding, arrhythmia, low cardiac output and neurological changes. • Early mobilisation when haemodynamically stable. Medical management Page 7 Cardiac Surgeries | Easy-language notes• Antiplatelet/statin/anti-anginal therapy when indicated after CABG. • Anticoagulation reversal after CPB with protamine as described in the source. • Inotropes may be required for low cardiac output. • Oxygen, fluids/electrolyte correction and rhythm treatment according to clinical need. For CABG, the source describes treatment of postoperative low cardiac output through oxygenation, preload/afterload optimisation, rhythm management and improved contractility; pharmacological or mechanical support may be needed. filecite turn12file4 L293-L313 Physiotherapy management • Positioning and frequent repositioning. • Diaphragmatic breathing and thoracic expansion exercises. • Supported cough/huff and secretion clearance when indicated. • Active ROM of limbs and ankle/foot exercises. • Early mobilisation: bed mobility → sitting → standing → walking. • Progressive walking and cardiac rehabilitation. • Monitor HR, BP, SpO2, RR, symptoms and exercise tolerance. • Stop/modify activity for new chest pain, marked breathlessness, dizziness/syncope, significant arrhythmia or haemodynamic instability. 10. QUICK EXAM REVISION Open-heart surgery: direct surgery on heart/great vessels, commonly through median sternotomy. CPB: temporarily takes over circulation + ventilation. CPB flow: venous blood → reservoir → oxygenator → pump → arterial system. Myocardial protection: cardioplegia, cross-clamp fibrillation, total circulatory arrest. CABG: bypasses coronary blockage with a graft; LIMA is important for LAD. Aneurysm: abnormal aortic dilatation; rupture may cause haemorrhage/tamponade. Tamponade: pericardial pressure compresses heart → reduced venous return/cardiac output; urgent drainage is required when unstable. CHD: structural heart abnormality present from birth. Shunt: abnormal blood pathway; left-to-right increases pulmonary flow, right-to-left may cause cyanosis.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-10T19:27:48+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
