ility where possible.\n\n\n**Analyzing Tool Selection**\n\nI've been refining my approach to tool selection based on those key instructions. Now, I explicitly consider all potential tools before choosing, prioritizing specific utilities over generic commands. The goal is to always identify the most efficient tool for the task to avoid using less efficient options when alternatives are available. I'm focusing on avoiding redundant or nested command structures.\n\n\n**Simplifying Cardiac Notes**\n\nI'm working on simplifying complex cardiac surgery notes for easier comprehension particularly for exam revision I'm focusing on Bailey & Love pages 823 to 849 I'm making sure to simplify the information and organize it clearly without the use of punctuation or other special characters I intend to create a structured outline that's easier to digest\n\n\n**Structuring Cardiac Notes**\n\nNow I'm creating a clear structure for the cardiac surgery notes, drawing from Bailey & Love pages 823 849. I'm treating the pages as a general study guide, not source material. I'm focusing on key topics like open-heart surgery, heart-lung bypass, congenital heart disease surgeries, CABG, aneurysm/aortic surgery, cardiac tamponade, heart transplant, cardiac shunts, general pre/post-op management, and quick exam revision. Each topic will have subtopics: Introduction, Types, Indications, Contraindications, Procedure, Complications, and Management. I'm also ensuring the language is easy, removing any unusual characters.\n\n\n**Refining Cardiac Formatting**\n\nI'm now focusing on formatting the cardiac surgery notes. I'm replacing hyphens with spaces for terms like \"open heart\" and \"heart lung.\" I've also identified and bolded the main topic headings, which include numbers, and added `---` before them for visual separation. Finally, I'm removing any unnecessary punctuation from the initial title text. I'm aiming for clean, easily digestible content.\n\n\n**Clarifying Symbol Usage**\n\nI'm no