{"step_index":7,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:01:05Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nBurns & Plastic Surgery — S. Das\n\nBPT Exam Notes | Easy language, moderately explained\n\nNote: These are simplified, exam-oriented notes based on standard surgical concepts covered in S. Das. They are paraphrased rather than copied verbatim.\n\n⸻\n\n1. BURNS\n\nDefinition\n\nA burn is an injury to the skin and sometimes deeper tissues caused by heat, chemicals, electricity, radiation or friction.\n\nEtiology\n\n* Thermal: flame, hot liquid, steam, hot objects.\n* Chemical: acids and alkalis.\n* Electrical: electrical current.\n* Radiation: sunlight, radiotherapy, etc.\n* Friction: skin injury due to rubbing.\n\nIndications for Hospital/Surgical Management\n\n* Deep or extensive burns.\n* Burns involving face, hands, feet, genitalia, major joints.\n* Circumferential burns.\n* Electrical or chemical burns.\n* Inhalation injury.\n* Burns requiring skin grafting.\n\nContraindications\n\nThere are no absolute contraindications to emergency burn treatment.\nDefinitive surgery may be delayed if the patient is haemodynamically unstable or medically unfit.\n\nRisk Factors\n\n* Children and elderly people.\n* Occupational exposure to fire/chemicals.\n* Smoking and unsafe cooking practices.\n* Poor electrical safety.\n* Lack of protective equipment.\n\nTypes\n\nAccording to depth:\n\n1. Superficial burn – epidermis involved; red and painful.\n2. Partial-thickness burn – epidermis and part of dermis involved; blistering and pain.\n3. Full-thickness burn – entire skin thickness destroyed; skin may be white, brown or charred and sensation may be reduced.\n4. Deep burn – extends into deeper tissues such as fat, muscle or bone.\n\nAccording to cause: thermal, chemical, electrical and radiation burns.\n\nInvestigation\n\n* Estimate percentage of total body surface area (TBSA) burned.\n* Assess burn depth.\n* Vital signs and urine output.\n* CBC, electrolytes, renal function.\n* ABG and carboxyhaemoglobin when inhalation injury is suspected.\n* ECG in electrical burns.\n* X-ray/other imaging when associated injuries are suspected.\n\nManagement\n\nImmediate management – ABCDE:\n\n* A: Airway assessment; early intubation if significant inhalation injury is suspected.\n* B: Oxygen and breathing support.\n* C: IV access and fluid resuscitation for significant burns.\n* D: Neurological assessment and pain control.\n* E: Remove burning source and clothing/jewellery; assess the whole body while preventing hypothermia.\n\nFurther management:\n\n* Fluid and electrolyte replacement.\n* Analgesics.\n* Wound cleaning and appropriate dressings.\n* Prevention/treatment of infection.\n* Nutrition and adequate protein intake.\n* Early excision and skin grafting for appropriate deep burns.\n* Physiotherapy and splinting to prevent contractures.\n\nComplications\n\n* Shock.\n* Infection and sepsis.\n* Inhalation injury.\n* Electrolyte imbalance.\n* Acute kidney injury.\n* Scarring and contractures.\n* Functional and cosmetic deformity.\n* Psychological problems.\n\n⸻\n\n2. RECONSTRUCTIVE SURGERY\n\nDefinition\n\nReconstructive surgery restores form and function of a body part damaged by trauma, burns, congenital defects, infection, tumour or previous surgery.\n\nEtiology / Indications\n\nUsed for:\n\n* Burn deformities.\n* Traumatic tissue loss.\n* Congenital deformities.\n* Post-tumour defects.\n* Chronic wounds.\n* Scar contractures.\n\nContraindications\n\n* Severe uncontrolled systemic illness.\n* Active uncontrolled infection.\n* Poor blood supply to the area.\n* Patient not medically fit for anaesthesia/surgery.\n\nRisk Factors for Poor Reconstruction\n\n* Smoking.\n* Diabetes.\n* Malnutrition.\n* Infection.\n* Poor vascular supply.\n* Poor wound healing.\n\nTypes\n\n* Skin grafts\n* Local flaps\n* Regional flaps\n* Free flaps\n* Tissue expansion\n* Reconstructive microsurgery\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\n1. Assess defect and surrounding tissue.\n2. Clean/debride unhealthy tissue.\n3. Select suitable reconstructive method.\n4. Transfer skin/tissue to cover the defect.\n5. Secure the graft/flap with sutures.\n6. Ensure adequate blood supply.\n7. Dress and monitor the reconstructed area.\n\nInvestigations\n\n* Clinical examination.\n* Assessment of vascular supply.\n* Blood tests.\n* Doppler studies when required.\n* Imaging for deeper or complex defects.\n\nManagement\n\n* Wound care.\n* Infection control.\n* Nutritional support.\n* Appropriate reconstructive surgery.\n* Physiotherapy and rehabilitation.\n* Scar and contracture management.\n\nComplications\n\n* Infection.\n* Bleeding/haematoma.\n* Poor wound healing.\n* Graft or flap failure.\n* Scar formation.\n* Contracture.\n* Functional or cosmetic deformity.\n\n⸻\n\n3. SKIN GRAFTS\n\nDefinition\n\nA skin graft is a piece of skin completely separated from its original blood supply and transferred to another area to cover a wound.\n\nIndications\n\n* Burns.\n* Large wounds.\n* Traumatic skin loss.\n* Ulcers.\n* Post-tumour defects.\n* Areas where primary closure is not possible.\n\nContraindications\n\n* Poorly vascularized wound bed.\n* Active uncontrolled infection.\n* Necrotic tissue.\n* Patient medically unfit for surgery.\n\nTypes\n\nAccording to thickness:\n\n1. Split-thickness skin graft (STSG) – epidermis and part of dermis.\n2. Full-thickness skin graft (FTSG) – epidermis and entire dermis.\n\nAccording to source:\n\n* Autograft: patient’s own skin.\n* Allograft: skin from another human.\n* Xenograft: skin from another species.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\n1. Prepare and clean recipient wound.\n2. Harvest skin from a suitable donor site.\n3. Prepare graft and recipient bed.\n4. Place graft over wound.\n5. Secure with sutures/staples.\n6. Apply dressing and immobilize the area.\n7. Monitor graft take.\n\nInvestigations\n\n* Wound assessment.\n* Vascularity of wound bed.\n* CBC, glucose and other routine preoperative investigations.\n\nManagement\n\n* Proper wound dressing.\n* Immobilization initially.\n* Monitor graft colour, warmth and adherence.\n* Treat infection if present.\n* After healing, moisturization, pressure therapy and physiotherapy may be used.\n\nComplications\n\n* Graft infection.\n* Haematoma/seroma.\n* Partial or complete graft loss.\n* Poor graft take.\n* Scar and contracture.\n* Donor-site infection or scarring.\n\n⸻\n\n4. COSMETIC / AESTHETIC SURGERY\n\nDefinition\n\nCosmetic surgery is surgery performed mainly to improve or alter physical appearance, rather than to restore function after disease or injury.\n\nIndications\n\n* Desired improvement in body or facial appearance.\n* Correction of selected cosmetic deformities.\n* Excess skin or localized fat.\n* Changes associated with ageing.\n\nContraindications\n\n* Unrealistic expectations.\n* Severe uncontrolled medical disease.\n* Active infection.\n* Poor psychological suitability.\n* Inability to understand risks and postoperative care.\n\nRisk Factors\n\n* Smoking.\n* Obesity.\n* Diabetes.\n* Poor nutrition.\n* Previous surgery/scarring.\n* Poor wound-healing tendency.\n\nTypes\n\n* Rhinoplasty.\n* Blepharoplasty.\n* Facelift.\n* Liposuction.\n* Abdominoplasty.\n* Breast augmentation/reduction.\n* Hair transplantation.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nDepends on the procedure. Generally:\nConsultation → examination → planning → anaesthesia → surgical correction → wound closure → postoperative monitoring.\n\nInvestigations\n\n* Medical and surgical history.\n* Physical examination.\n* Routine blood investigations.\n* ECG/imaging when indicated.\n\nManagement\n\n* Preoperative counselling.\n* Appropriate surgical procedure.\n* Pain and wound management.\n* Prevention of infection.\n* Gradual return to activity.\n\nComplications\n\n* Bleeding.\n* Infection.\n* Anaesthetic complications.\n* Scarring.\n* Asymmetry.\n* Nerve injury.\n* Poor cosmetic result.\n* Thromboembolic complications in selected operations.\n\n⸻\n\n5. CONTRACTURE AND SCAR MANAGEMENT\n\nDefinition\n\nA scar is fibrous tissue formed during wound healing.\nA contracture occurs when scar tissue shortens and restricts movement, commonly after burns.\n\nEtiology\n\n* Deep burns.\n* Poorly healed wounds.\n* Trauma.\n* Infection.\n* Surgery.\n* Prolonged immobilization.\n\nIndications for Treatment\n\n* Restricted joint movement.\n* Pain or itching.\n* Functional limitation.\n* Significant cosmetic deformity.\n* Hypertrophic or problematic scars.\n\nContraindications\n\nFor conservative treatment, there are few absolute contraindications.\nSurgery may be delayed in active infection or when the patient is medically unfit.\n\nRisk Factors\n\n* Deep burns.\n* Large wounds.\n* Healing by secondary intention.\n* Infection.\n* Poor rehabilitation.\n* Scar crossing a joint.\n\nTypes of Scars\n\n* Normal scar\n* Hypertrophic scar\n* Keloid\n* Contracture scar\n\nInvestigation\n\nMainly clinical examination:\n\n* Scar size, thickness and mobility.\n* Joint ROM.\n* Functional assessment.\n* Vascular/nerve status when required.\n\nManagement\n\nConservative:\n\n* Pressure garments.\n* Silicone gel/sheets.\n* Moisturization and massage.\n* Stretching and ROM exercises.\n* Splinting/positioning.\n* Physiotherapy.\n\nSurgical:\n\n* Scar release.\n* Z-plasty.\n* Skin grafting.\n* Local/regional/free flap reconstruction when required.\n\nComplications\n\n* Restricted ROM.\n* Deformity.\n* Pain and itching.\n* Recurrent contracture.\n* Functional disability.\n* Psychological/cosmetic problems.\n\n⸻\n\n6. MICROVASCULAR / MICROSURGICAL PROCEDURES\n\nDefinition\n\nMicrosurgery is surgery performed using magnification and fine instruments to repair or join very small blood vessels and nerves.\n\nIndications\n\n* Free flap reconstruction.\n* Replantation of amputated parts.\n* Complex traumatic defects.\n* Head and neck reconstruction.\n* Breast reconstruction.\n* Limb reconstruction.\n\nContraindications\n\n* Patient medically unfit.\n* Severe uncontrolled infection.\n* Inadequate recipient vessels.\n* Severe peripheral vascular disease in selected cases.\n\nRisk Factors\n\n* Smoking.\n* Diabetes.\n* Vascular disease.\n* Infection.\n* Poor nutrition.\n* Excessive tension on the reconstructed vessels.\n\nTypes\n\n* Free tissue transfer/free flap\n* Microvascular arterial repair.\n* Microvascular venous repair.\n* Nerve repair.\n* Replantation.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\n1. Prepare recipient and donor sites.\n2. Harvest tissue with its artery and vein.\n3. Transfer tissue to the defect.\n4. Under microscope, artery and vein are joined (anastomosis).\n5. Blood flow is restored.\n6. Flap is monitored for adequate circulation.\n7. Wound is closed and postoperative monitoring is continued.\n\nInvestigations\n\n* Clinical vascular examination.\n* Doppler/Doppler ultrasound.\n* CT angiography or other vascular imaging when needed.\n* Routine preoperative blood investigations.\n\nManagement\n\n* Maintain adequate circulation and oxygenation.\n* Keep the reconstructed limb/area appropriately positioned.\n* Frequent flap monitoring.\n* Avoid compression of the vascular pedicle.\n* Early detection of vascular compromise.\n* Rehabilitation after stabilization.\n\nComplications\n\n* Arterial or venous thrombosis.\n* Flap congestion.\n* Flap ischemia and necrosis.\n* Infection.\n* Bleeding/haematoma.\n* Anastomotic failure.\n* Nerve dysfunction.\n* Partial or complete flap loss.\n\n⸻\n\n⭐ Quick Revision\n\nBurns:\nABCDE → fluids → wound care → infection prevention → excision/grafting → physiotherapy\n\nReconstructive surgery:\nDefect assessment → debridement → graft/flap → wound closure → rehabilitation\n\nSkin graft:\nHarvest skin → prepare wound → place graft → secure → dressing → monitor graft take\n\nContracture:\nStretching + splinting + pressure/silicone → scar care → surgical release if required\n\nMicrosurgery:\nHarvest tissue → transfer → microscopic vessel anastomosis → restore blood flow → monitor flap//////////////////////////////////////////// Absolutely. Below are ENT notes in the same order as your cardiac-surgery answer: Definition → Etiology → Indications → Contraindications → Risk factors → Types → Surgical procedure → Investigations → Management → Complications, in easy, exam-oriented language, suitable for BPT university exams and broadly aligned with S. Das surgical approach.\n\nENT — EAR, NOSE AND THROAT\n\n⸻\n\n1. ANATOMY AND PHYSIOLOGY OF HEARING\n\nDefinition\n\nHearing is the ability to perceive sound through the ear and interpret it by the brain.\n\nAnatomy of Ear\n\nThe ear is divided into 3 parts:\n\nA. External ear\n\t•\tPinna/auricle\n\t•\tExternal auditory canal\n\t•\tTympanic membrane\n\nFunction: Collects and conducts sound waves toward the middle ear.\n\nB. Middle ear\n\nContains:\n\t•\tTympanic cavity\n\t•\tMalleus\n\t•\tIncus\n\t•\tStapes\n\t•\tEustachian tube\n\nFunction: Transmits and amplifies sound vibrations from tympanic membrane to the inner ear.\n\nC. Inner ear\n\nContains:\n\t•\tCochlea\n\t•\tVestibule\n\t•\tSemicircular canals\n\nCochlea → hearing\nVestibule + semicircular canals → balance\n\nPhysiology of Hearing\n\nSound pathway:\n\nSound waves → Pinna → External auditory canal → Tympanic membrane → Malleus → Incus → Stapes → Oval window → Cochlea → Organ of Corti → Auditory nerve → Brain\n\nMechanism\n\t1.\tPinna collects sound.\n\t2.\tSound travels through external auditory canal.\n\t3.\tTympanic membrane vibrates.\n\t4.\tOssicles transmit and amplify vibration.\n\t5.\tStapes moves at oval window.\n\t6.\tFluid movement occurs inside cochlea.\n\t7.\tHair cells of Organ of Corti are stimulated.\n\t8.\tMechanical energy becomes electrical nerve impulses.\n\t9.\tImpulses travel through CN VIII (vestibulocochlear nerve).\n\t10.\tBrain interprets the impulses as sound.\n\n⸻\n\n2. AUDIOMETRY\n\nDefinition\n\nAudiometry is a method of measuring a person’s hearing ability using different sound frequencies and intensities.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tHearing loss\n\t•\tDeafness\n\t•\tTinnitus\n\t•\tVertigo\n\t•\tSuspected ear disease\n\t•\tPre-operative assessment\n\t•\tOccupational hearing assessment\n\t•\tHearing-aid selection\n\nTypes\n\n1. Pure Tone Audiometry (PTA)\n\nMeasures hearing thresholds for different frequencies.\n\n2. Speech Audiometry\n\nMeasures:\n\t•\tSpeech detection\n\t•\tSpeech recognition/discrimination\n\n3. Impedance Audiometry\n\nIncludes:\n\t•\tTympanometry\n\t•\tAcoustic reflex testing\n\n4. Bone-conduction audiometry\n\nTests sound conduction through skull bones.\n\n5. Air-conduction audiometry\n\nTests the complete hearing pathway through the external and middle ear.\n\nProcedure\n\t•\tPatient sits in a quiet/soundproof room.\n\t•\tHeadphones are placed over the ears.\n\t•\tDifferent frequencies and intensities of sound are given.\n\t•\tPatient indicates when the sound is heard.\n\t•\tHearing threshold is recorded.\n\nInterpretation\n\nConductive hearing loss\n\nProblem in:\n\t•\tExternal ear\n\t•\tMiddle ear\n\nAir conduction is poorer than bone conduction.\n\nSensorineural hearing loss\n\nProblem in:\n\t•\tCochlea\n\t•\tAuditory nerve\n\nAir and bone conduction are both reduced.\n\nMixed hearing loss\n\nCombination of:\n\t•\tConductive loss\n\t•\tSensorineural loss\n\nManagement\n\nDepends on cause:\n\t•\tMedical treatment\n\t•\tSurgery\n\t•\tHearing aid\n\t•\tCochlear implant\n\t•\tCommunication training\n\n⸻\n\n3. OTITIS MEDIA\n\nDefinition\n\nOtitis media is inflammation or infection of the middle ear.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tBacterial infection\n\t•\tViral infection\n\t•\tUpper respiratory tract infection\n\t•\tEustachian tube dysfunction\n\t•\tAllergy\n\t•\tAdenoid enlargement\n\nCommon organisms include:\n\t•\tStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\t•\tHaemophilus influenzae\n\t•\tMoraxella catarrhalis\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\nSurgery may be required in:\n\t•\tRecurrent otitis media\n\t•\tPersistent middle-ear fluid\n\t•\tChronic suppurative otitis media\n\t•\tCholesteatoma\n\t•\tComplications\n\nContraindications\n\nThere is no absolute contraindication to necessary surgery. Elective surgery may be postponed with:\n\t•\tAcute uncontrolled infection\n\t•\tSevere medical illness\n\t•\tUnfit for anaesthesia\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tChildren\n\t•\tRecurrent URTI\n\t•\tDay-care exposure\n\t•\tPassive smoking\n\t•\tBottle feeding while lying down\n\t•\tAdenoid hypertrophy\n\t•\tEustachian tube abnormalities\n\nTypes\n\n1. Acute otitis media\n\nSudden infection and inflammation.\n\n2. Otitis media with effusion\n\nFluid is present without acute infection.\n\n3. Chronic suppurative otitis media\n\nPersistent/recurrent infection with ear discharge and perforation.\n\nClinical Features\n\t•\tEar pain\n\t•\tFever\n\t•\tHearing loss\n\t•\tEar discharge\n\t•\tIrritability in children\n\t•\tFeeling of blocked ear\n\t•\tTinnitus\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tOtoscopy\n\t•\tAudiometry\n\t•\tTympanometry\n\t•\tCT temporal bone in complicated disease\n\t•\tCulture of discharge when required\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAnalgesics\n\t•\tAppropriate antibiotics when bacterial infection is suspected\n\t•\tTreat nasal/upper-airway problems\n\t•\tMyringotomy\n\t•\tGrommet/tympanostomy tube\n\t•\tMastoid surgery for selected chronic disease\n\nComplications\n\t•\tHearing loss\n\t•\tTympanic membrane perforation\n\t•\tMastoiditis\n\t•\tCholesteatoma\n\t•\tFacial nerve palsy\n\t•\tLabyrinthitis\n\t•\tMeningitis\n\t•\tBrain abscess\n\n⸻\n\n4. SINUSITIS\n\nDefinition\n\nSinusitis is inflammation of the mucosa of one or more paranasal sinuses.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tViral infection\n\t•\tBacterial infection\n\t•\tAllergy\n\t•\tNasal polyps\n\t•\tDeviated nasal septum\n\t•\tDental infection\n\t•\tPoor sinus drainage\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\t•\tChronic sinusitis despite medical treatment\n\t•\tNasal polyps\n\t•\tObstructed sinus drainage\n\t•\tRecurrent sinusitis\n\t•\tComplications\n\t•\tAnatomical obstruction\n\nContraindications\n\nRelative:\n\t•\tUncontrolled systemic disease\n\t•\tActive severe infection requiring stabilization\n\t•\tUnfit for anaesthesia\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tAllergy\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tNasal obstruction\n\t•\tDeviated septum\n\t•\tNasal polyps\n\t•\tRecurrent URTI\n\t•\tImmunodeficiency\n\nTypes\n\nAccording to duration\n\t1.\tAcute sinusitis\n\t2.\tSubacute sinusitis\n\t3.\tChronic sinusitis\n\t4.\tRecurrent acute sinusitis\n\nAccording to sinus involved\n\t•\tMaxillary\n\t•\tFrontal\n\t•\tEthmoidal\n\t•\tSphenoidal\n\t•\tPansinusitis\n\nClinical Features\n\t•\tNasal blockage\n\t•\tFacial pain/pressure\n\t•\tHeadache\n\t•\tNasal discharge\n\t•\tPostnasal drip\n\t•\tReduced smell\n\t•\tFever\n\t•\tCough\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tClinical examination\n\t•\tNasal endoscopy\n\t•\tX-ray in selected cases\n\t•\tCT scan of paranasal sinuses\n\t•\tAllergy testing when indicated\n\nManagement\n\t•\tSaline nasal irrigation\n\t•\tAnalgesics\n\t•\tIntranasal corticosteroids\n\t•\tAntibiotics when bacterial infection is suspected\n\t•\tTreat allergy\n\t•\tEndoscopic sinus surgery when indicated\n\nComplications\n\t•\tOrbital cellulitis\n\t•\tOrbital abscess\n\t•\tOsteomyelitis\n\t•\tMeningitis\n\t•\tBrain abscess\n\t•\tCavernous sinus thrombosis\n\n⸻\n\n5. MASTOID SURGERY\n\nDefinition\n\nMastoid surgery (mastoidectomy) is an operation in which infected or diseased mastoid air cells are removed.\n\nEtiology / Indications\n\nUsually performed because of:\n\t•\tChronic suppurative otitis media\n\t•\tCholesteatoma\n\t•\tMastoiditis\n\t•\tPersistent ear infection\n\t•\tComplications of middle-ear disease\n\nTypes\n\n1. Simple/cortical mastoidectomy\n\nDiseased mastoid air cells are removed while preserving important structures.\n\n2. Modified radical mastoidectomy\n\nDisease is removed while attempting to preserve useful hearing structures when possible.\n\n3. Radical mastoidectomy\n\nMore extensive removal of diseased tissue; used for extensive disease.\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tPatient medically unfit for anaesthesia\n\t•\tUncontrolled systemic illness\n\t•\tAcute conditions requiring stabilization\n\nRisk Factors for Mastoid Disease\n\t•\tChronic otitis media\n\t•\tRecurrent ear infection\n\t•\tCholesteatoma\n\t•\tPoor Eustachian tube function\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tOtoscopy\n\t•\tAudiometry\n\t•\tTympanometry\n\t•\tCT temporal bone\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\t•\tCulture of discharge\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t1.\tGeneral anaesthesia.\n\t2.\tIncision is made behind the ear.\n\t3.\tMastoid bone is exposed.\n\t4.\tDiseased mastoid air cells are drilled out.\n\t5.\tInfection/cholesteatoma is removed.\n\t6.\tImportant structures are preserved where possible.\n\t7.\tWound is closed and dressed.\n\nManagement\n\nPost-operative\n\t•\tAntibiotics when indicated\n\t•\tAnalgesics\n\t•\tWound care\n\t•\tKeep ear dry\n\t•\tFollow-up examination\n\t•\tAudiological assessment\n\t•\tGradual return to activity\n\nComplications\n\t•\tFacial nerve injury\n\t•\tHearing loss\n\t•\tDizziness/vertigo\n\t•\tCSF leak\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tTaste disturbance\n\t•\tRecurrence of disease\n\n⸻\n\n6. TRACHEOSTOMY\n\nDefinition\n\nTracheostomy is a surgical procedure in which an opening is made in the anterior wall of the trachea and a tube is inserted to maintain an airway.\n\nIndications\n\nA. Upper airway obstruction\n\t•\tTumor\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tForeign body\n\t•\tSevere edema\n\nB. Prolonged mechanical ventilation\n\nC. Airway protection\n\t•\tNeurological disorders\n\t•\tReduced consciousness\n\t•\tPoor cough\n\nD. Removal of secretions\n\nWhen the patient cannot clear secretions effectively.\n\nContraindications\n\nThere are few absolute contraindications in an emergency.\n\nRelative:\n\t•\tUncorrected bleeding disorder\n\t•\tSevere anatomical abnormality\n\t•\tLocal infection\n\t•\tUnstable patient\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tBleeding disorders\n\t•\tObesity\n\t•\tPrevious neck surgery\n\t•\tNeck tumors\n\t•\tAnatomical abnormalities\n\t•\tEmergency procedure\n\nTypes\n\nAccording to duration\n\t•\tTemporary\n\t•\tPermanent\n\nAccording to site\n\t•\tUpper tracheostomy\n\t•\tMiddle tracheostomy\n\t•\tLower tracheostomy\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t1.\tPatient is positioned appropriately.\n\t2.\tAnaesthesia is given.\n\t3.\tNeck is exposed and incision is made.\n\t4.\tTrachea is identified.\n\t5.\tOpening is made in tracheal rings.\n\t6.\tTracheostomy tube is inserted.\n\t7.\tTube is secured.\n\t8.\tOxygen/ventilation is connected as required.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tAirway examination\n\t•\tOxygen saturation\n\t•\tABG\n\t•\tChest X-ray when indicated\n\t•\tCBC\n\t•\tCoagulation profile\n\nManagement\n\nTracheostomy care\n\t•\tMaintain tube patency\n\t•\tSuction secretions when required\n\t•\tHumidification\n\t•\tStoma care\n\t•\tCuff-pressure monitoring when cuffed tube is used\n\t•\tOxygen therapy\n\t•\tChest physiotherapy\n\t•\tCommunication support\n\t•\tSwallowing assessment\n\nPhysiotherapy\n\t•\tPositioning\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tChest expansion\n\t•\tAirway clearance\n\t•\tSuctioning when trained/indicated\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\nComplications\n\nEarly\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tPneumothorax\n\t•\tTube displacement\n\t•\tAirway obstruction\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tSubcutaneous emphysema\n\nLate\n\t•\tTracheal stenosis\n\t•\tTracheoesophageal fistula\n\t•\tGranulation tissue\n\t•\tDifficulty decannulation\n\t•\tScar formation\n\n⸻\n\n7. HEARING AIDS\n\nDefinition\n\nA hearing aid is an electronic device that amplifies and modifies sound to help a person with hearing loss hear better.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tMild to severe hearing loss\n\t•\tConductive hearing loss when surgery is unsuitable\n\t•\tSensorineural hearing loss\n\t•\tMixed hearing loss\n\t•\tAge-related hearing loss\n\nContraindications / Limitations\n\t•\tUntreated active ear infection\n\t•\tSevere ear discharge until treated\n\t•\tProfound hearing loss where hearing aid provides inadequate benefit\n\t•\tPatient unable to use/manage the device\n\nTypes\n\n1. Behind-the-ear (BTE)\n\nDevice sits behind the ear.\n\n2. In-the-ear (ITE)\n\nFits within the outer ear.\n\n3. In-the-canal (ITC)\n\nFits partly inside ear canal.\n\n4. Completely-in-canal (CIC)\n\nFits almost completely inside canal.\n\nComponents\n\t•\tMicrophone\n\t•\tAmplifier/processor\n\t•\tReceiver/speaker\n\t•\tBattery/power source\n\nWorking\n\nSound → microphone → amplifier/processor → receiver → ear\n\nInvestigations before fitting\n\t•\tOtoscopy\n\t•\tPure tone audiometry\n\t•\tSpeech audiometry\n\t•\tHearing-loss assessment\n\t•\tEar mould assessment when required\n\nManagement\n\t•\tSelect appropriate hearing aid\n\t•\tProper fitting\n\t•\tProgramming\n\t•\tPatient education\n\t•\tRegular cleaning\n\t•\tBattery/device care\n\t•\tFollow-up hearing assessment\n\nComplications/Problems\n\t•\tEar irritation\n\t•\tFeedback/whistling\n\t•\tPoor sound quality\n\t•\tImproper fitting\n\t•\tEar blockage sensation\n\t•\tPoor compliance\n\n⸻\n\n8. FACIAL NERVE PALSY SURGERIES\n\nDefinition\n\nFacial nerve palsy is weakness or paralysis of the muscles supplied by the facial nerve (CN VII).\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tBell’s palsy\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tTemporal bone fracture\n\t•\tTumor\n\t•\tEar disease\n\t•\tMastoid surgery\n\t•\tParotid surgery\n\t•\tInfections\n\t•\tCongenital causes\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\nSurgery may be considered in:\n\t•\tSevere nerve injury\n\t•\tComplete paralysis with poor recovery\n\t•\tTraumatic nerve transection\n\t•\tPersistent paralysis\n\t•\tFacial deformity\n\t•\tEye protection problems\n\t•\tLong-standing paralysis\n\nTypes of Surgery\n\t•\tFacial nerve decompression in selected cases\n\t•\tDirect nerve repair\n\t•\tNerve grafting\n\t•\tNerve transfer\n\t•\tMuscle transfer\n\t•\tStatic facial suspension\n\t•\tEyelid procedures for corneal protection\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere uncontrolled systemic illness\n\t•\tPatient unfit for anaesthesia\n\t•\tPoor surgical prognosis in selected cases\n\nRisk Factors\n\nDepend on cause:\n\t•\tHead trauma\n\t•\tEar surgery\n\t•\tTumors\n\t•\tSevere ear infections\n\t•\tTemporal bone fracture\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tComplete facial nerve examination\n\t•\tAudiometry\n\t•\tElectroneurography\n\t•\tElectromyography\n\t•\tCT temporal bone\n\t•\tMRI brain/internal auditory canal when indicated\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nDepends on the cause:\n\t1.\tIdentify damaged nerve.\n\t2.\tRemove compression if present.\n\t3.\tRepair nerve directly if possible.\n\t4.\tUse nerve graft if there is a gap.\n\t5.\tNerve transfer or muscle transfer may be used in chronic cases.\n\t6.\tEye-protection surgery may be performed when required.\n\nManagement\n\t•\tProtect the eye\n\t•\tLubricating eye drops\n\t•\tEye patch/taping when indicated\n\t•\tFacial exercises\n\t•\tNeuromuscular retraining\n\t•\tMassage where appropriate\n\t•\tSpeech/swallowing therapy if affected\n\t•\tSurgical correction when required\n\nComplications\n\t•\tPersistent facial weakness\n\t•\tIncomplete eye closure\n\t•\tCorneal injury\n\t•\tSynkinesis\n\t•\tFacial asymmetry\n\t•\tDifficulty eating/speaking\n\t•\tPsychological problems\n\n⸻\n\n9. VERTIGO\n\nDefinition\n\nVertigo is a false sensation of movement, usually feeling that the person or surroundings are spinning.\n\nEtiology\n\nPeripheral causes\n\t•\tBenign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV)\n\t•\tMénière disease\n\t•\tVestibular neuritis\n\t•\tLabyrinthitis\n\t•\tOtitis media\n\nCentral causes\n\t•\tStroke\n\t•\tBrain tumor\n\t•\tMultiple sclerosis\n\t•\tCerebellar disease\n\t•\tMigraine\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\nSurgery is reserved for selected severe conditions such as:\n\t•\tPersistent Ménière disease\n\t•\tCertain tumors\n\t•\tSevere structural disease\n\t•\tVertigo not controlled by conservative treatment\n\nContraindications\n\nDepend on the proposed surgery and patient’s medical fitness.\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tIncreasing age\n\t•\tPrevious ear disease\n\t•\tHead trauma\n\t•\tMigraine\n\t•\tVascular disease\n\t•\tCertain medications\n\nTypes\n\nPeripheral vertigo\n\nOriginates from the inner ear/vestibular nerve.\n\nCentral vertigo\n\nOriginates from the brainstem or cerebellum.\n\nClinical Features\n\t•\tSpinning sensation\n\t•\tNausea/vomiting\n\t•\tLoss of balance\n\t•\tNystagmus\n\t•\tSweating\n\t•\tDifficulty walking\n\t•\tHearing symptoms in some peripheral disorders\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tVestibular examination\n\t•\tDix-Hallpike test\n\t•\tHead impulse test\n\t•\tAudiometry\n\t•\tTympanometry when appropriate\n\t•\tVNG/ENG\n\t•\tMRI brain when central cause is suspected\n\t•\tCT when indicated\n\nManagement\n\nBPPV\n\t•\tCanalith repositioning manoeuvre, e.g. Epley manoeuvre\n\t•\tVestibular exercises\n\nOther causes\n\t•\tTreat underlying disease\n\t•\tVestibular rehabilitation\n\t•\tMedicines for selected patients\n\t•\tSurgery in selected severe cases\n\nPhysiotherapy\n\t•\tVestibular adaptation\n\t•\tGaze stabilization\n\t•\tBalance training\n\t•\tHabituation exercises\n\t•\tGait training\n\t•\tFall prevention\n\nComplications\n\t•\tFalls\n\t•\tInjury\n\t•\tAnxiety\n\t•\tReduced mobility\n\t•\tDifficulty performing ADLs\n\t•\tPersistent imbalance\n\n⸻\n\n10. OTOSCLEROSIS\n\nDefinition\n\nOtosclerosis is an abnormal fixation/remodelling of the bones of the middle ear, especially the stapes, causing progressive conductive hearing loss.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tAbnormal bone remodeling\n\t•\tGenetic/familial factors\n\t•\tSometimes associated with hormonal influences\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\t•\tSignificant conductive hearing loss\n\t•\tProgressive hearing loss\n\t•\tPatient wants improved hearing\n\t•\tGood surgical candidate\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tActive ear infection\n\t•\tSevere medical illness\n\t•\tPoor surgical fitness\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tFamily history\n\t•\tFemale sex\n\t•\tGenetic predisposition\n\t•\tPregnancy may influence progression in some patients\n\nTypes\n\t•\tFenestral otosclerosis\n\t•\tCochlear/retrofenestral otosclerosis\n\nClinical Features\n\t•\tProgressive hearing loss\n\t•\tUsually conductive initially\n\t•\tTinnitus\n\t•\tUsually no ear discharge\n\t•\tVertigo may occur in some cases\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tOtoscopy\n\t•\tPure tone audiometry\n\t•\tSpeech audiometry\n\t•\tTympanometry\n\t•\tCT temporal bone in selected cases\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nStapedectomy/Stapedotomy\n\t1.\tMiddle ear is accessed.\n\t2.\tFixed stapes is treated.\n\t3.\tA small opening is made in the footplate in stapedotomy.\n\t4.\tProsthesis is inserted to transmit sound to the inner ear.\n\nManagement\n\t•\tObservation in mild cases\n\t•\tHearing aid\n\t•\tStapedotomy/stapedectomy when indicated\n\t•\tRegular audiological follow-up\n\nComplications\n\t•\tSensorineural hearing loss\n\t•\tVertigo\n\t•\tTinnitus\n\t•\tTaste disturbance\n\t•\tFacial nerve injury\n\t•\tProsthesis displacement\n\t•\tPersistent hearing loss\n\n⸻\n\n11. APHONIA\n\nDefinition\n\nAphonia is complete loss of the ability to produce a normal voice.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tLaryngeal disease\n\t•\tVocal cord paralysis\n\t•\tLaryngeal tumor\n\t•\tSevere inflammation\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tNeurological disorders\n\t•\tFunctional/psychogenic causes\n\t•\tExcessive voice use\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\nSurgery may be required for:\n\t•\tVocal cord paralysis\n\t•\tTumor\n\t•\tStructural vocal cord lesion\n\t•\tSevere airway/voice abnormality\n\nContraindications\n\nDepend on the planned operation and general medical fitness.\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tExcessive voice use\n\t•\tLaryngeal infection\n\t•\tVocal cord trauma\n\t•\tNeck/chest surgery\n\t•\tNeurological disease\n\nTypes\n\t•\tOrganic aphonia\n\t•\tFunctional/psychogenic aphonia\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tLaryngoscopy\n\t•\tFlexible nasolaryngoscopy\n\t•\tStroboscopy\n\t•\tImaging when indicated\n\t•\tNeurological examination\n\t•\tVoice assessment\n\nManagement\n\t•\tVoice rest\n\t•\tTreatment of underlying disease\n\t•\tSpeech/voice therapy\n\t•\tVocal hygiene\n\t•\tSurgery when indicated\n\t•\tPsychological management for functional aphonia\n\nComplications\n\t•\tPersistent voice loss\n\t•\tCommunication difficulty\n\t•\tSocial/occupational problems\n\t•\tAspiration in associated vocal cord paralysis\n\t•\tPsychological distress\n\n⸻\n\n12. DEAFNESS\n\nDefinition\n\nDeafness is partial or complete loss of the ability to hear sounds.\n\nEtiology\n\nConductive causes\n\t•\tWax\n\t•\tOtitis media\n\t•\tTympanic membrane perforation\n\t•\tOssicular problems\n\t•\tOtosclerosis\n\nSensorineural causes\n\t•\tCochlear damage\n\t•\tNoise exposure\n\t•\tAging\n\t•\tOtotoxic drugs\n\t•\tGenetic causes\n\t•\tViral infections\n\t•\tAuditory nerve disorders\n\nMixed\n\nCombination of conductive and sensorineural hearing loss.\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\nDepending on cause:\n\t•\tChronic ear disease\n\t•\tOtosclerosis\n\t•\tCholesteatoma\n\t•\tOssicular damage\n\t•\tSevere conductive loss\n\t•\tCochlear implantation for selected severe/profound sensorineural loss\n\nContraindications\n\nDepend on procedure. Examples:\n\t•\tActive infection for elective implant surgery\n\t•\tSevere medical illness\n\t•\tAnatomical limitations\n\t•\tInability to undergo anaesthesia\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tNoise exposure\n\t•\tIncreasing age\n\t•\tEar infections\n\t•\tOtotoxic medications\n\t•\tHead trauma\n\t•\tGenetic factors\n\t•\tCongenital infections\n\t•\tPrematurity and certain neonatal complications\n\nTypes\n\n1. Conductive hearing loss\n\nProblem in external or middle ear.\n\n2. Sensorineural hearing loss\n\nProblem in inner ear or auditory nerve.\n\n3. Mixed hearing loss\n\nBoth conductive and sensorineural components.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tOtoscopy\n\t•\tPure tone audiometry\n\t•\tSpeech audiometry\n\t•\tTympanometry\n\t•\tBone-conduction testing\n\t•\tOtoacoustic emissions\n\t•\tAuditory brainstem response\n\t•\tCT/MRI when indicated\n\nManagement\n\nConductive\n\t•\tRemove wax\n\t•\tTreat infection\n\t•\tTympanoplasty\n\t•\tOssicular surgery\n\t•\tStapedotomy when appropriate\n\nSensorineural\n\t•\tHearing aid\n\t•\tCochlear implant in selected severe/profound cases\n\t•\tCommunication training\n\t•\tTreatment of underlying cause\n\nRehabilitation\n\t•\tAuditory training\n\t•\tSpeech therapy\n\t•\tLip-reading/sign-language support when appropriate\n\t•\tAssistive listening devices\n\nComplications\n\t•\tCommunication difficulties\n\t•\tDelayed speech/language development in children\n\t•\tSocial isolation\n\t•\tEducational difficulties\n\t•\tReduced quality of life\n\t•\tBalance problems in associated vestibular disease //////////////////// Sure. Below are Circulatory Surgeries in the same order, with an easy, exam-oriented format: definition → etiology → indications → contraindications → risk factors → types → surgical procedure → investigations → management → complications.\n\nCirculatory Surgeries & Related Conditions\n\n1. Embolectomy\n\nDefinition\n\nEmbolectomy is a surgical procedure to remove an embolus (blood clot or other material) blocking a blood vessel and restore blood flow.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tBlood clot from heart or large vessels\n\t•\tAtrial fibrillation\n\t•\tRecent surgery\n\t•\tDeep vein thrombosis\n\t•\tTrauma to blood vessels\n\t•\tProsthetic heart valves\n\nIndications\n\t•\tAcute arterial blockage\n\t•\tSevere limb ischemia\n\t•\tSudden loss of circulation\n\t•\tThreatened limb\n\t•\tPulmonary embolism in selected severe cases\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere irreversible ischemic damage\n\t•\tVery small/distal vessel obstruction unsuitable for removal\n\t•\tPatient unfit for surgery\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tAtherosclerosis\n\t•\tAtrial fibrillation\n\t•\tDVT\n\t•\tImmobilization\n\t•\tRecent surgery\n\t•\tHypercoagulable states\n\t•\tSmoking\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tArterial embolectomy – removal of embolus from an artery.\n\t2.\tPulmonary embolectomy – removal of clot from pulmonary arteries.\n\t3.\tCatheter embolectomy – clot removed using a balloon catheter.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tVessel is exposed.\n\t•\tVessel is opened.\n\t•\tA catheter may be passed beyond the clot.\n\t•\tBalloon is inflated and withdrawn.\n\t•\tClot is removed.\n\t•\tBlood flow is checked.\n\t•\tVessel is closed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tDoppler ultrasound\n\t•\tCT angiography\n\t•\tMR angiography\n\t•\tECG\n\t•\tBlood coagulation tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAnticoagulants\n\t•\tPain control\n\t•\tTreatment of underlying cause\n\t•\tLimb elevation when appropriate\n\t•\tEarly mobilization after stabilization\n\nComplications\n\t•\tRe-embolism\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tVessel injury\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tTissue ischemia\n\t•\tCompartment syndrome\n\n⸻\n\n2. Vascular Reconstruction\n\nDefinition\n\nVascular reconstruction is surgery performed to repair, replace, or bypass a damaged or blocked blood vessel to restore normal circulation.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tAtherosclerosis\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tAneurysm\n\t•\tCongenital vascular abnormalities\n\t•\tThrombosis\n\nIndications\n\t•\tSevere arterial blockage\n\t•\tTraumatic vessel injury\n\t•\tAneurysm\n\t•\tCritical limb ischemia\n\t•\tVascular occlusion\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere infection at operative site\n\t•\tVery poor general condition\n\t•\tIrreversible tissue damage\n\t•\tUnsuitable distal vessels\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tDiabetes\n\t•\tHypertension\n\t•\tHyperlipidemia\n\t•\tObesity\n\t•\tOlder age\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tBypass grafting\n\t2.\tEndarterectomy\n\t3.\tVascular graft/interposition\n\t4.\tPatch angioplasty\n\t5.\tAngioplasty and stenting\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tBlocked/damaged vessel is identified.\n\t•\tA graft may be taken from the patient or an artificial graft may be used.\n\t•\tBlood is diverted around the obstruction.\n\t•\tGraft is connected above and below the blockage.\n\t•\tBlood flow is checked.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tDoppler\n\t•\tCT angiography\n\t•\tConventional angiography\n\t•\tABI\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAntiplatelet/anticoagulant therapy\n\t•\tControl diabetes and BP\n\t•\tSmoking cessation\n\t•\tWound care\n\t•\tExercise rehabilitation\n\nComplications\n\t•\tGraft thrombosis\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tRe-occlusion\n\t•\tPoor wound healing\n\t•\tLimb ischemia\n\n⸻\n\n3. Aneurysm Surgery\n\nDefinition\n\nAn aneurysm is an abnormal localized dilatation or ballooning of a blood vessel due to weakness of its wall.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tAtherosclerosis\n\t•\tHypertension\n\t•\tCongenital weakness\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tInfection\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\t•\tLarge aneurysm\n\t•\tRapidly increasing size\n\t•\tSymptomatic aneurysm\n\t•\tRisk of rupture\n\t•\tRuptured aneurysm\n\nContraindications\n\nUsually relative and depend on the patient’s surgical risk:\n\t•\tSevere cardiac disease\n\t•\tSevere respiratory disease\n\t•\tUncontrolled infection\n\t•\tVery poor general condition\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tHypertension\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tAtherosclerosis\n\t•\tOlder age\n\t•\tFamily history\n\t•\tMale sex\n\nTypes\n\t•\tAbdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)\n\t•\tThoracic aortic aneurysm\n\t•\tPeripheral artery aneurysm\n\t•\tCerebral aneurysm\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nTwo common approaches:\n\n1. Open repair\n\t•\tAneurysm is exposed.\n\t•\tBlood flow is controlled.\n\t•\tAffected segment is replaced with a graft.\n\n2. Endovascular repair\n\t•\tCatheter is introduced through an artery.\n\t•\tA stent-graft is placed inside the vessel.\n\t•\tBlood flows through the graft instead of the aneurysm.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tUltrasound\n\t•\tCT angiography\n\t•\tMRI\n\t•\tAngiography\n\nManagement\n\t•\tBP control\n\t•\tSmoking cessation\n\t•\tRegular monitoring for small aneurysms\n\t•\tSurgical/endovascular repair when indicated\n\nComplications\n\t•\tRupture\n\t•\tSevere internal bleeding\n\t•\tThrombosis\n\t•\tOrgan ischemia\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tDeath\n\n⸻\n\n4. Amputation\n\nDefinition\n\nAmputation is the surgical removal of a part or whole limb when it cannot be preserved or is dangerous to the patient.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tSevere peripheral vascular disease\n\t•\tGangrene\n\t•\tDiabetes\n\t•\tSevere trauma\n\t•\tMalignancy\n\t•\tSevere infection\n\nIndications\n\t•\tIrreversible gangrene\n\t•\tNon-healing infected wound\n\t•\tSevere ischemia\n\t•\tSevere trauma\n\t•\tMalignant tumor\n\t•\tLife-threatening infection\n\nContraindications\n\nThere are generally no absolute contraindications when amputation is life-saving, but surgery may be delayed for:\n\t•\tUncontrolled shock\n\t•\tSevere infection requiring stabilization\n\t•\tPoor general condition\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tDiabetes\n\t•\tPeripheral arterial disease\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tPoor wound healing\n\t•\tTrauma\n\nTypes\n\nAccording to level:\n\t•\tToe amputation\n\t•\tTransmetatarsal\n\t•\tBelow-knee amputation\n\t•\tAbove-knee amputation\n\t•\tHip disarticulation\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tDiseased tissue is removed.\n\t•\tHealthy tissue is preserved as much as possible.\n\t•\tBone is cut at an appropriate level.\n\t•\tMuscles are balanced and attached.\n\t•\tNerves are handled carefully.\n\t•\tSkin is closed to form a stump.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tDoppler\n\t•\tAngiography\n\t•\tX-ray\n\t•\tBlood sugar\n\t•\tCBC\n\t•\tInfection markers\n\nManagement\n\t•\tPain control\n\t•\tWound care\n\t•\tPrevention of contractures\n\t•\tStump care\n\t•\tStrengthening exercises\n\t•\tBalance and gait training\n\t•\tProsthetic training\n\nComplications\n\t•\tPhantom limb sensation/pain\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tStump pain\n\t•\tJoint contracture\n\t•\tPoor wound healing\n\t•\tPsychological problems\n\n⸻\n\n5. Gangrene Surgery\n\nDefinition\n\nGangrene is the death of body tissue due to loss of blood supply, infection, or both.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tSevere arterial obstruction\n\t•\tDiabetes\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tFrostbite\n\t•\tSevere burns\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\t•\tDead tissue\n\t•\tSpreading infection\n\t•\tWet gangrene\n\t•\tSepsis\n\t•\tSevere irreversible ischemia\n\nContraindications\n\nSurgery may be temporarily delayed until the patient is stabilized in:\n\t•\tSevere shock\n\t•\tUncontrolled metabolic abnormalities\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tDiabetes\n\t•\tPeripheral vascular disease\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tImmunosuppression\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tDry gangrene – mainly due to loss of blood supply.\n\t2.\tWet gangrene – dead tissue with infection.\n\t3.\tGas gangrene – caused by gas-producing bacteria.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nDepending on severity:\n\t•\tDebridement – removal of dead tissue\n\t•\tDrainage of infection\n\t•\tRevascularization\n\t•\tAmputation if tissue cannot be saved\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tDoppler\n\t•\tX-ray\n\t•\tCT/MRI when required\n\t•\tBlood culture\n\t•\tCBC\n\t•\tBlood glucose\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAntibiotics for infection\n\t•\tRevascularization when possible\n\t•\tDebridement\n\t•\tBlood sugar control\n\t•\tAmputation when necessary\n\nComplications\n\t•\tSepsis\n\t•\tSeptic shock\n\t•\tTissue loss\n\t•\tAmputation\n\t•\tMulti-organ failure\n\t•\tDeath\n\n⸻\n\n6. Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)\n\nDefinition\n\nDVT is the formation of a blood clot inside a deep vein, usually in the leg.\n\nEtiology\n\nDVT commonly occurs due to Virchow’s triad:\n\t1.\tVenous stasis\n\t2.\tVessel wall injury\n\t3.\tIncreased blood clotting\n\nIndications for Treatment\n\t•\tConfirmed DVT\n\t•\tPrevention of clot extension\n\t•\tPrevention of pulmonary embolism\n\nContraindications to Anticoagulation\n\t•\tActive major bleeding\n\t•\tSevere bleeding disorder\n\t•\tRecent certain types of major surgery\n\t•\tSevere thrombocytopenia\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tProlonged bed rest\n\t•\tLong-distance travel\n\t•\tSurgery\n\t•\tPregnancy\n\t•\tCancer\n\t•\tObesity\n\t•\tPrevious DVT\n\t•\tImmobilization\n\nTypes\n\t•\tProximal DVT – femoral/iliac veins\n\t•\tDistal DVT – calf veins\n\t•\tUpper-limb DVT\n\nSurgical/Interventional Treatment\n\nUsually DVT is treated medically rather than by routine surgery.\n\nIn severe selected cases:\n\t•\tCatheter-directed thrombolysis\n\t•\tThrombectomy\n\t•\tInferior vena cava filter when anticoagulation cannot be used and PE risk is significant\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tDuplex Doppler ultrasound\n\t•\tD-dimer\n\t•\tCT/MR venography in selected cases\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAnticoagulants\n\t•\tCompression therapy when appropriate\n\t•\tEarly mobilization after adequate treatment\n\t•\tTreat underlying cause\n\t•\tPrevention of recurrence\n\nComplications\n\t•\tPulmonary embolism\n\t•\tPost-thrombotic syndrome\n\t•\tChronic venous insufficiency\n\t•\tRecurrent DVT\n\n⸻\n\n7. Pulmonary Embolism (PE)\n\nDefinition\n\nPulmonary embolism occurs when a blood clot blocks a pulmonary artery, usually after travelling from a DVT in the leg.\n\nEtiology\n\nMost commonly:\nDVT → clot travels → pulmonary artery → obstruction\n\nIndications for Treatment\n\t•\tConfirmed PE\n\t•\tPrevention of further clot formation\n\t•\tSevere PE causing hemodynamic instability\n\nContraindications\n\nDepend on treatment:\n\t•\tAnticoagulation: active major bleeding\n\t•\tThrombolysis: major bleeding risk or ce